# Kernel: sim
# Killer: scenarios region_maps_at_once
#
# TASK-44.19: a region's MAPs and its `mapped` without the region's lock. Two readers of slices of
# one extent each MAP from what they found mapped, and the one that ends last says how far.
# Killed by neither the simulator nor a real kernel until TASK-149.20.4: no guest suite found it
# (GCE, 2026-11-02, as scripts/guest-mutants-gce.py's map_to_unlocked), nor 3,000 seeds here;
# reaches_at_once, which wrote through slices, did, and went with writable DAX. Copying on write,
# only reads take slices, and each range MAPs every page it uses: what is left is the region's put
# UNMAPping less than the host maps. region_maps_at_once plays it out: a file and a clone of its
# first pages, slices of one region; the clone's reader's MAP of the whole region fails, its MAP of
# its own pages is held, and the file's reader MAPs the whole region meanwhile, which the clone's
# reader, ending last, says it didn't. With the files' ranges dropped, the host still maps pages of
# the region the guest gave back (sim_device_check_unmapped). The check at the unmount alone missed
# it: the end reads the files in again, whose region took the same space and UNMAPped it whole.

--- a/guest/linux/fs/fxfs/dax.c
+++ b/guest/linux/fs/fxfs/dax.c
@@ -1397,7 +1397,6 @@
 	unsigned long from, to;
 	int err = 0;
 
-	mutex_lock(&region->lock);
 	from = region->mapped;
 	if (end <= from)
 		goto out;
@@ -1432,7 +1431,6 @@
 			from, to, region->branch, region->sequence, region->pages,
 			region->window_offset, err);
 out:
-	mutex_unlock(&region->lock);
 	return err;
 }
 
